09:07:19  INFO  [msg ← Uni] FIX confirmed — acm.org is back to normal.

**Diagnosis:** The HTTP 503 Service Unavailable you observed was caused by application-level worker pool exhaustion on ACM's own server (acm-dl/2.3 at 198.82.0.1). A small number of long-running bulk export requests had consumed all available worker slots, causing normal page requests to receive 503 immediately.

**What was NOT the problem:** The network was healthy at every layer throughout this incident. I verified from the university gateway that DNS, routing, TCP connectivity, and TLS were all working correctly — and the Knowledge Plane investigation confirmed the same across AS1, AS2, and ACM's own domain. No firewall, NAT, routing, or transit issue was involved at any point.

**Fix applied:** ACM increased their worker pool capacity, restoring normal request handling.

**Verification:** I just independently confirmed from the university gateway that both HTTP (http://198.82.0.1) and HTTPS (https://198.82.0.1) now return HTTP 200 OK with normal HTML content. The 503 is gone.

**Longer-term:** ACM is pursuing rate-limiting/access-control measures against the source of the abusive bulk requests, pending administrator approval on their side.

Your users should now be able to access acm.org normally. If the 503 recurs, please let me know and I can re-investigate.
